{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Daniel Mercer blinked awake to the harsh glow of fluorescent lights that pulsed in time with his heartbeat. He sat up, fingers digging into the stiff mattress, only to realize the sheet beneath him wasn’t cotton—it was some kind of pixelated fabric, shimmering faintly like a low-poly facade. His breath hitched as he glanced down: his hands, smooth and human, trembled above a keyboard that shouldn’t have been there. The clock on his nightstand read *06:00:42*, but the numbers flickered like a glitching display. A distant hum filled the air, and when he turned his head, the walls of his bedroom stretched seamlessly into an endless void, as if his room were just another texture in an infinite world. His stomach twisted. He wasn’t in his apartment anymore. He was inside the game.\n\nThe first clue came when Daniel tried to stand. His legs moved, but his body stuttered—like a frame skipped—before snapping back into place. He stumbled to the window, expecting to see his neighborhood, but the glass reflected a distorted version of himself, his edges jagged and pixelated. Outside, the sky wasn’t blue; it was a gradient of shifting colors, cycling through hues like a corrupted loading screen. Then he heard the *sound*: a faint, repetitive *beep-beep-beep*, the signature tune of his childhood RPG, *Chronos Legends*. His pulse pounded in his ears as he spotted the menu bar hovering at the bottom of his vision—*Health: 100/100 • Ammo: 0/0 • Quests: None*. His hands shook as he reached for the imaginary controller in his mind. If this was a game, then who—or what—was the player?", "logprobs": [{"token": "Daniel", "logprob": -1.6451058387756348}, {"token": " Mercer", "logprob": -0.29138293862342834}, {"token": " blinked", "logprob": -2.080463171005249}, {"token": " awake", "logprob": -1.6756092309951782}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.3655073046684265}, 